{
 "artifact_type": "omp_ninfer_measurement",
 "schema_version": 1,
 "experiment": "EXP-060",
 "title": "RTX 5090 new sessions wait on the GPU's idle P-state: the first prefill pass after 9 s idle runs 1.6-2.3x slower",
 "generated_utc": "2026-09-26T10:11:13Z",
 "status": "passed",
 "question": "Why do production's new agent sessions (the shared-stable-prefix warm turns EXP-059 left untraced) take 0.315 s of prefill for 140 computed tokens, and what would shorten them without changing an output byte?",
 "answer": "They start on an idle GPU. In production's v0.7.0 agent traffic 103 of 106 new sessions, and 138 of 446 agent requests, arrived 5 s or more after the previous request finished; new sessions arriving that late took 0.317 s of prefill (TTFT 0.361 s), the three that arrived sooner 0.171 s (TTFT 0.199 s), and continuations after 5 s idle 0.347 s for 201 tokens against 0.240 s for 446 on a busy GPU. After the last response the RTX 5090 steps from P1 to P3 at about 2.0 s, P5 at about 7.4 s and P8 (270 MHz SM, 405 MHz memory) at about 9.2 s. On production's binary with a 30.2K-token shared prefix, back-to-back new sessions of 159-230 computed tokens prefilled in 0.156-0.160 s (TTFT 0.167-0.191 s): about 5-15 ms of state bookkeeping, one prompt pass of 123-127 ms and two small-T fragments. After 12-30 s idle the same sessions took 0.253-0.334 s (TTFT 0.317-0.393 s) because their prompt pass ran 205-287 ms with every kernel family slower, while the fragments after it ran at normal speed once the clocks had ramped. A side process launching a 0.4 ms kernel every second changed neither the P-state steps nor the slowdown.",
 "decision": "Open, measure only: nothing built or shipped; production stays on v0.8.1. The lever keeps output bytes, since clock state does not change arithmetic: a GPU held out of its low idle states when a request arrives would take production's new-session TTFT from about 0.36 s to about 0.19 s and post-idle continuations down by about 0.15 s. Two routes, neither measured yet: a host clock floor (nvidia-smi locked clocks on the Windows host, a global setting with an idle-power cost), or a keep-warm load for a grace period after each request, which needs more than a 1 Hz 0.4 ms kernel; the smallest load that holds P3 and its power are unmeasured.",
 "method": {
  "production": "production v0.7.0 request logs on the RTX 5090, agent requests (11 or more tools, 446): idle time before a request is its start (finish time minus total time) minus the previous request's finish, grouped at 5 s; kinds are cold (16K+ computed tokens), middle, and warm by reuse path.",
  "probe_windows": "2026-09-26, RTX 5090, production held and stopped each time and restored healthy: 09:41-09:43Z (back-to-back), 09:53-09:55Z (idle gaps of 0, 12, 0, 30, 0 and 12 s before sessions 1-6), 10:00-10:03Z (the same gaps with a side container launching a single-warp 1,000,000-cycle spin kernel every second). Each served production's image and binary (5b2f2471) with production's arguments under Nsight Systems 2026.1.3 (seccomp unconfined): one cold request whose system message (~30K tokens of public omp-ninfer documentation) and 12 tool definitions form the automatic stable prefix, then six new two-message sessions with a ~140-token first user message, greedy, 64 output tokens.",
  "sampling": "nvidia-smi from WSL every 50 ms (about 19 samples per second): P-state, SM and memory clocks; per-pass GPU spans from the program.submit NVTX ranges; per-request host timelines from the engine's admission, context-progress and commit ranges with the copies they launched."
 },
